RELATIONAL OPERATORS
Relational operators in Java are used to compare two values (operands) to determine the relationship between them. 
These operators always return a boolean value: either true or false. 
They are fundamental for decision-making structures like if-else statements and loops like for and while loop.
The relational operators are: =, ==, >, <, >=, <=, !=
  
  NOTE: 
  1. '=' is used to assign values to variables, whereas '==' is used exclusively for comparison.
  2. Relational operators can also be used to compare primitive data types like floating-point numbers(float, double) and characters(char) based on their numeric ASCII/Unicode values.
  3. '==' need to be avoided to compare the actual text inside two String objects, as it compares their memory references rather than values. Instead ' .equals()' method can be used.

EXAMPLE:
public class relationaloperators 
{
    public static void main(String args[])
    {
//<,>,<=,>=,==,!= are the various relational operators and the return type is boolean i.e true or false
//long and double 8 bytes  float is 4 bytes
double k1 = 8.125;
double k2 =  8.569;
double k3 = 8.135;
boolean result = k1 > k3;
System.out.println(result);
   }
}
